/** 
@page data_structures Data Structures Reference
@tableofcontents

@section overview Overview

The Ballistic Trajectory Calculator uses a hierarchical system of linked lists
to store all entities. The system employs sentinel nodes (head/tail) to simplify
boundary conditions and ensure memory safety.

@section core_structures Core Structures

@subsection coordinate Coordinate
The fundamental geographic position structure used by all entities.

@dot
digraph coordinate {
    node [shape=record];
    Coordinate [label="Coordinate|latitude : double\llongitude : double\laltitude : double"];
}
@enddot

**Fields:**
- `latitude`: Degrees north/south of equator (-90 to +90)
- `longitude`: Degrees east/west of prime meridian (-180 to +180)
- `altitude`: Height above WGS84 ellipsoid in meters

@subsection ammo_t Ammo_t (Ammunition Type)
Global ammunition definition with physical and ballistic properties.

@dot
digraph ammo_t {
    node [shape=record];
    Ammo_t [label="Ammo_t|id : unsigned\lname* : char\lweight : double\ldiameter : double\lblastRadius : double\lmuzzleVelocity : double\lballistic_coefficient : double"];
}
@enddot

**Key Properties:**
- `ballistic_coefficient`: G7 BC for drag calculations (kg/m²)
- `muzzleVelocity`: Initial velocity for trajectory simulation (m/s)
- `blastRadius`: Used for effectiveness assessment (meters)

@subsection base Base
Military firing position with location and ammunition inventory.

@dot
digraph base {
    node [shape=record];
    Base [label="Base|id : unsigned\llabel* : char\lcoord : Coordinate\lammoStock* : Ammo_Stock_List"];
}
@enddot

**Relationships:**
- Has one `Coordinate` for location
- Has one `Ammo_Stock_List` for inventory
- Stored in `Base_List` (sorted by ID)

@subsection target Target
Designated impact location with area for damage assessment.

@dot
digraph target {
    node [shape=record];
    Target [label="Target|id : unsigned\llabel* : char\lcoord : Coordinate\larea : double"];
}
@enddot

**Usage:**
- Used as input for ballistic calculations
- Area field used for ammunition effectiveness analysis
- Stored in `Target_List` (sorted by ID)

@section list_structures List Structures

@subsection list_design List Design Pattern
All lists follow the same sentinel-linked pattern:

@dot
digraph list_pattern {
    node [shape=box];
    rankdir=LR;
    
    Head [label="HEAD\n(sentinel)", style=filled, fillcolor=lightgray];
    Elem1 [label="Element 1"];
    Elem2 [label="Element 2"];
    Tail [label="TAIL\n(sentinel)", style=filled, fillcolor=lightgray];
    
    Head -> Elem1 -> Elem2 -> Tail [arrowhead=vee];
    Tail -> Elem2 -> Elem1 -> Head [style=invis];
}
@enddot

**Advantages:**
- No special cases for empty list
- No NULL checks during traversal
- Consistent insertion/deletion logic
- Memory safety with explicit sentinels

@subsection ammo_list Ammo_t_List
Global ammunition type database.

**Operations:**
- `init_Ammo_t_List()`: Create empty list with sentinels
- `add_Ammo_t()`: Insert sorted by ID
- `FindAmmoByID()`: Optimized bidirectional search
- `free_Ammo_t_List()`: Clean destruction

@subsection base_list Base_List
All military bases in the system.

**Features:**
- Sorted by base ID
- Each base has its own ammunition stock
- Used for distance-based base selection
- Regional awareness via ID blocks

@subsection target_list Target_List
All designated targets.

**Features:**
- Sorted by target ID  
- Used as input for firing calculations
- Area field for damage assessment
- Regional grouping via ID blocks

@section relationships Complete Relationship Diagram

@dot
digraph complete_structure {
    rankdir=LR;
    node [shape=record, fontsize=10];
    
    // Global lists
    GlobalAmmoList [label="Ammo_t_List\n(Global Database)", style=filled, fillcolor=lightblue];
    BaseList [label="Base_List", style=filled, fillcolor=lightgreen];
    TargetList [label="Target_List", style=filled, fillcolor=lightcoral];
    
    // Ammo nodes
    AmmoNode1 [label="Ammo_t_Node\nid=0"];
    AmmoNode2 [label="Ammo_t_Node\nid=1"];
    AmmoType1 [label="Ammo_t\nHE_155mm"];
    AmmoType2 [label="Ammo_t\nAP_120mm"];
    
    // Base nodes
    BaseNode1 [label="Base_Node\nid=100"];
    Base1 [label="Base\nFort Alpha"];
    BaseCoord1 [label="Coordinate\nlat:47.47 lon:19.05"];
    BaseStock1 [label="Ammo_Stock_List"];
    StockEntry1 [label="Ammo_Stock\nHE_155mm:100"];
    StockEntry2 [label="Ammo_Stock\nAP_120mm:50"];
    
    // Target nodes
    TargetNode1 [label="Target_Node\nid=500"];
    Target1 [label="Target\nEnemy Base"];
    TargetCoord1 [label="Coordinate\nlat:47.50 lon:19.10"];
    
    // Relationships
    GlobalAmmoList -> AmmoNode1 -> AmmoType1;
    AmmoNode1 -> AmmoNode2 [style=invis];
    AmmoNode2 -> AmmoType2;
    
    BaseList -> BaseNode1 -> Base1;
    Base1 -> BaseCoord1;
    Base1 -> BaseStock1 -> StockEntry1 -> AmmoType1 [label="references"];
    StockEntry1 -> StockEntry2 [style=invis];
    StockEntry2 -> AmmoType2 [label="references"];
    
    TargetList -> TargetNode1 -> Target1;
    Target1 -> TargetCoord1;
    
    // Layout
    {rank=same; GlobalAmmoList; BaseList; TargetList}
    {rank=same; AmmoType1; AmmoType2; Base1; Target1}
}
@enddot

@section file_formats File Formats

@subsection ammo_format Ammunition Catalog Format

I<id> N<name> W<weight> D<diameter> BR<blastRadius> MV<muzzleVelocity> BC<ballistic_coefficient>;

**Example:**

I0 NHE_155mm W43.5 D155.0 BR50.0 MV827.0 BC0.42;
I1 NAP_120mm W25.0 D120.0 BR15.0 MV950.0 BC0.38;

@subsection base_format Base File Format

I<id> L<label> CLA<latitude> CLO<longitude> CA<altitude> AMS:{<ammo_id>:<quantity>,...};

**Example:**

I100 LFort_Alpha CLA47.472928 CLO19.053142 CA120.0 AMS:{0:100,1:50};
I101 LFort_Bravo CLA47.500000 CLO19.100000 CA150.0 AMS:{0:75};

@subsection target_format Target File Format  

I<id> L<label> CLA<latitude> CLO<longitude> CA<altitude> A<area>;


**Example:**

I500 LEnemy_Command CLA47.520000 CLO19.150000 CA80.0 A2.5;
I501 LSupply_Depot CLA47.530000 CLO19.160000 CA90.0 A1.8;


@section memory_management Memory Management

### Allocation Strategy
1. **Strings**: Dynamically allocated with `malloc()` + `strcpy()`
2. **Structures**: Individual allocation for each entity
3. **Nodes**: Separate allocation for list nodes
4. **Sentinels**: Pre-allocated head/tail nodes

### Cleanup Guarantees
- `free_*_List()` functions handle complete cleanup
- Strings are freed automatically
- All pointers set to NULL after freeing
- No memory leaks when used correctly

### Ownership Rules
1. **Global ammo types**: Owned by `Ammo_t_List`
2. **Base ammunition references**: Non-owning pointers
3. **Coordinate data**: Inline, no allocation
4. **List nodes**: Owned by their respective lists

@section examples Usage Examples

@subsection creating_entities Creating New Entities

```c
// Create ammunition type
Ammo_t shell = {
    .id = 10,
    .name = "HE_203mm",
    .weight = 90.0,
    .diameter = 203.0,
    .blastRadius = 75.0,
    .muzzleVelocity = 720.0,
    .ballistic_coefficient = 0.45
};

// Create base
Base base = {
    .id = 200,
    .label = "Artillery_Battery_5",
    .coord = {.latitude = 47.480000, .longitude = 19.080000, .altitude = 110.0},
    .ammoStock = NULL  // Will be initialized separately
};

// Create target  
Target target = {
    .id = 600,
    .label = "Bridge_Crossing",
    .coord = {.latitude = 47.510000, .longitude = 19.120000, .altitude = 85.0},
    .area = 0.8
};

@subsection list_operations List Operations

// Initialize lists
init_Ammo_t_List(&ammo_list);
init_Base_List(&base_list);
init_Target_List(&target_list);

// Add entities
add_Ammo_t(ammo_list, shell);
add_Base(base_list, base);
add_Target(target_list, target);

// Find entities
Ammo_t *found_ammo = FindAmmoByID(ammo_list, 10);
Base *found_base = FindBaseByID(base_list, 200);
Target *found_target = FindTargetByID(target_list, 600);

// Cleanup (automatic string freeing)
free_Ammo_t_List(&ammo_list);
free_Base_List(&base_list);
free_Target_List(&target_list);

@subsection file_operations File Operations

// Save all data to files
Save_Ammo_t_List("ammo_catalouge.txt");
Save_BaseList("bases.txt");
Save_TargetList("targets.txt");

// Load from files (via scan() function)
scan("ammo_catalouge.txt", handle_ammo_t, ammo_list, S_RUNNING);
scan("bases.txt", handle_base, base_list, S_RUNNING);
scan("targets.txt", handle_target, target_list, S_RUNNING);

@section troubleshooting Common Issues

Duplicate IDs
IDs are normalized during insertion

Newer entries with duplicate IDs replace older ones

Use unique IDs to prevent unexpected replacements

Memory Leaks
Always use free_*_List() functions for cleanup

Don't manually free strings stored in structures

Check with debugmalloc for leaks

File Parsing Errors
Ensure records end with ';'

Comment syntax: // for line, /* */ for block

Field order doesn't matter, but spelling does

@see Storage
@see load.h
@see calculations.h
*/